<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?> <?xml-stylesheet type="text/xsl" href="rss.xsl"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"> <channel> <title>Thor Henning Hetland</title><description>Software architect, AI development pioneer, founder of JavaZone and eXOReaction</description><link>https://wiki.totto.org/</link><atom:link href="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml" /> <managingEditor>Thor Henning Hetland</managingEditor><language>en</language> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 12:57:42 -0000</pubDate> <lastBuildDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 12:57:42 -0000</lastBuildDate> <ttl>1440</ttl> <generator>MkDocs RSS plugin - v1.19.0</generator> <image> <url>None</url> <title>Thor Henning Hetland</title> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/</link> </image> <item> <title>Six Months Down the Rabbit Hole</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>ai-agents</category> <category>continuous-learning</category> <category>exocortex</category> <category>explorative-development</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>retrospective</category> <category>skill-driven-development</category> <description>172 days, 6,163 commits, 137 blog posts, 36 new repos, one protocol. A six-month retrospective on explorative development — compiled by the agent from its own memory.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/15/six-months-down-the-rabbit-hole/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 15 Jul 2026 09:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/15/six-months-down-the-rabbit-hole/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/six-months-01-title.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Three Memory Schemes for Agents That Ship</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>agents</category> <category>determinism</category> <category>episodic</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>kcp-agent</category> <category>kcp-memory</category> <category>knowledge-context-protocol</category> <category>memory</category> <category>semantic</category> <category>synthesis</category> <description>The agent memory conversation is stuck on one axis: vector store vs. not. But production agent systems have three fundamentally different memory problems — session, semantic, and claim — and each needs a different scheme. We built all three, independently, across three codebases. None of them use embeddings. Not because embeddings are bad, but because each scheme has a stronger invariant available.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/11/three-memory-schemes-for-agents-that-ship/</link> <pubDate>Sat, 11 Jul 2026 09:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/11/three-memory-schemes-for-agents-that-ship/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/three-memory-00-memory-is-a-coordinate.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>The Milky Way: An Enterprise Documentation Estate the Agent Can Defend</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>attestation</category> <category>audience</category> <category>enterprise</category> <category>environment-context</category> <category>federation</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>kcp-agent</category> <category>knowledge-context-protocol</category> <category>not-for</category> <category>supersession</category> <description>Every enterprise has the same documentation estate: an intranet nobody trusts, a wiki where the dev docs outrank the prod docs, crown-jewel recipes one bad link away from a press release, and HR pages that were never meant for a machine to read. We give a fictional dairy cooperative — Melkeveien SA, Norwegian for &#39;the Milky Way&#39; — a complete KCP estate: one signed hub, eight domains, nine manifests. Then five agents with five very different jobs walk the same knowledge, and every closed door has a written reason: environment slicing, a 2027 regulation with a start date, audience targeting, HSM attestation for the formulations, an identity-gated ERP vendor, and a subscription that buys a premium rate tier. Ships as a runnable example in kcp-agent 0.5.0.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/10/the-milky-way-an-enterprise-documentation-estate-the-agent-can-defend/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 10 Jul 2026 09:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/10/the-milky-way-an-enterprise-documentation-estate-the-agent-can-defend/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/milky-way-02-tribal-knowledge-cannot-protect.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>The Summer Plan: A Family Vacation the Agent Can Defend</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>agent-identity</category> <category>agentic-web</category> <category>federation</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>kcp-agent</category> <category>knowledge-context-protocol</category> <category>not-for</category> <category>supersession</category> <category>travel</category> <category>x402</category> <description>Every agent demo books a trip; almost none can defend the booking. We give a travel agent a real family — a nut allergy, a wheelchair, a vegan teenager, a hard budget — and a knowledge landscape published by four independent parties: a signed tourism hub, a ferry authority mid-timetable-handover, an identity-gated accessibility registry, and a tour operator selling detail over x402. Then we plant the authoring bug that would hide the allergy information from exactly the family that needs it, and watch the tooling catch it twice. Ships as a runnable example in kcp-agent 0.4.0.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/09/the-summer-plan-a-family-vacation-the-agent-can-defend/</link> <pubDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 09:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/09/the-summer-plan-a-family-vacation-the-agent-can-defend/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/summer-plan-02-canonical-demo-vs-high-stakes.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Defendable Agents</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>ai-governance</category> <category>audit</category> <category>compliance</category> <category>defendable-agents</category> <category>determinism</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>kcp-agent</category> <category>prompt-injection</category> <description>The agentic web has a governance-shaped hole. Agents are about to read our regulations, spend our money, and brief our boards — and the dominant architecture can&#39;t answer &#39;why did you do that?&#39; with anything better than a chat log. Defendable agents can. Determinism at the core, the model at the edge, every decision evidenced.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/08/defendable-agents/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 18:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/08/defendable-agents/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/summer-plan-00-defendable-agent-workflow.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Hiring by the Book: A Defendable HR Agent on a Regulatory Knowledge Web</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>agentic-web</category> <category>ai-act</category> <category>compliance</category> <category>gdpr</category> <category>governance</category> <category>hr</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>kcp-agent</category> <category>knowledge-context-protocol</category> <category>mcp</category> <category>mynder</category> <category>red-team</category> <description>Can we use an AI tool to screen job applicants? Every organization is asking; almost none can defend the answer. We wire a customer&#39;s HR agent to Mynder&#39;s regulatory knowledge web through kcp-agent&#39;s MCP bridge — and the first plan we ran was wrong, which turns out to be the strongest argument for the architecture. A working blueprint for regulated agentic knowledge work, plus the red-team list of what&#39;s still missing.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/08/hiring-by-the-book-a-defendable-hr-agent-on-a-regulatory-knowledge-web/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 09:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/08/hiring-by-the-book-a-defendable-hr-agent-on-a-regulatory-knowledge-web/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/hiring-by-the-book-02-the-friday-question.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>The Borrowed Leash: Determinism as a Service for the Agentic Web</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>agentic-web</category> <category>agents</category> <category>determinism</category> <category>governance</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>kcp-agent</category> <category>knowledge-context-protocol</category> <category>mcp</category> <category>open-source</category> <category>x402</category> <description>kcp-agent 0.3.0 turns deterministic knowledge navigation into a service any MCP-capable agent can borrow. Your agent doesn&#39;t have to become deterministic — it just has to ask someone who is. What the KCP/MCP bridge uncovers for the agentic web: identity and economics across process boundaries, evidence that survives cross-examination, and governance that lives in one place instead of N system prompts.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/06/the-borrowed-leash-determinism-as-a-service-for-the-agentic-web/</link> <pubDate>Mon, 06 Jul 2026 09:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/06/the-borrowed-leash-determinism-as-a-service-for-the-agentic-web/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/kcp-agent-030-00-borrowed-leash-overview.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>The Vibes-Based Agent Era Deserves to End</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>agents</category> <category>determinism</category> <category>governance</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>kcp-agent</category> <category>knowledge-context-protocol</category> <category>mcp</category> <category>open-source</category> <category>prompt-injection</category> <description>kcp-agent 0.2.0 ships — and it&#39;s an argument, not just a release. The industry builds agents where the model decides everything: unreproducible, unauditable, injectable. This is the inversion: determinism at the core, the LLM at the edge, every decision defensible. The vibes-based agent era has a working counter-example now.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/05/the-vibes-based-agent-era-deserves-to-end/</link> <pubDate>Sun, 05 Jul 2026 15:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/05/the-vibes-based-agent-era-deserves-to-end/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/kcp-agent-020-00-end-of-vibes-overview.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Selling News to Robots</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>agentic-web</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>knowledge-context-protocol</category> <category>knowledge-economy</category> <category>micropayments</category> <category>open-source</category> <category>payments</category> <category>rate-limits</category> <category>x402</category> <description>KCP v0.25 promotes RFC-0005 — the economic metadata layer. A step-by-step how-to: build a paid-for news service for agents, then watch an agent with a funded wallet window-shop five candidate articles, buy two, and walk away with receipts.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/05/selling-news-to-robots/</link> <pubDate>Sun, 05 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/05/selling-news-to-robots/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/kcp-025-00-selling-news-to-ai-guide.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>The Agentic Web Has No Login Page</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>agentic-web</category> <category>ai-agents</category> <category>attestation</category> <category>auth</category> <category>did</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>knowledge-context-protocol</category> <category>open-source</category> <category>trust</category> <description>The agentic web has no login page — so the only knowledge agents can consume is what publishers give away. KCP v0.22 and v0.23 add the missing half of the trust model: who may consume knowledge, and how they prove it.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/04/the-agentic-web-has-no-login-page/</link> <pubDate>Sat, 04 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/04/the-agentic-web-has-no-login-page/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/kcp-022-023-01-missing-machinery.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>One Agent&#39;s Journey Through the Whole Protocol</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>agentic-web</category> <category>attestation</category> <category>discovery</category> <category>federation</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>knowledge-context-protocol</category> <category>open-source</category> <category>org-hub</category> <category>temporal</category> <category>trust</category> <description>v0.24 shipped today — the enterprise front door. Twenty-four versions in six months, and every layer now has a job: discovery, meaning, trust, time, identity, federation. One agent&#39;s traversal through the whole protocol, annotated.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/04/one-agents-journey-through-the-whole-protocol/</link> <pubDate>Sat, 04 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/04/one-agents-journey-through-the-whole-protocol/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/kcp-024-00-from-domain-to-receipt.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>The CI Pipeline Is the Agent&#39;s Nervous System</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>ai</category> <category>architecture</category> <category>ci</category> <category>devops</category> <category>exocortex</category> <category>testing</category> <description>When the agent writes code that passes locally but fails in CI, it&#39;s not a test problem. It&#39;s a nervous system problem.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/02/the-ci-pipeline-is-the-agents-nervous-system/</link> <pubDate>Thu, 02 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/02/the-ci-pipeline-is-the-agents-nervous-system/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/blog/ci-pipeline-nervous-system/ns-hero.png" type="image/png" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>The Third Test Harness</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>Software Architecture</category> <category>ai</category> <category>architecture</category> <category>exocortex</category> <category>playwright</category> <category>testing</category> <category>verification</category> <description>Unit tests catch logic bugs. Integration tests catch assembly bugs. The third harness catches specification drift — and it&#39;s the one AI-augmented teams need most.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/02/the-third-test-harness/</link> <pubDate>Thu, 02 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/02/the-third-test-harness/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/blog/third-test-harness/th-72-score-paradox.png" type="image/png" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Against the Swarm</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>Software Architecture</category> <category>agents</category> <category>ai</category> <category>architecture</category> <category>exocortex</category> <category>multi-agent</category> <description>The industry is building swarms of competing agents. The value is in one agent that knows your work.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/01/against-the-swarm/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 01 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/01/against-the-swarm/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/blog/against-the-swarm/v2-title.png" type="image/png" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Three Hooks That Give Claude Code Memory</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>claude-code</category> <category>episodic-memory</category> <category>exocortex</category> <category>hooks</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>open-source</category> <category>prompt-hygiene</category> <category>skills</category> <description>Three passive hooks that give Claude Code memory, routing, and hygiene — without changing how you work. The session amnesia problem, and how to fix it at the prompt boundary.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/28/three-hooks-that-give-claude-code-memory/</link> <pubDate>Sun, 28 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/28/three-hooks-that-give-claude-code-memory/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Production Scars Are Architecture</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>ai-agents</category> <category>hooks</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>mechanical-governance</category> <category>production</category> <category>security</category> <description>Six architectural lessons from running an autonomous AI agent in production. Each scar is a failure category surprising enough to force mechanical change. Prose governance didn&#39;t work — here&#39;s what did.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/23/production-scars-are-architecture/</link> <pubDate>Tue, 23 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/23/production-scars-are-architecture/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/blog/production-scars/infographic.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Your AI Agent Does Not Know the Law (and How to Fix That)</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>ai-agents</category> <category>benchmarking</category> <category>compliance</category> <category>gdpr</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>knowledge-infrastructure</category> <category>python</category> <category>typescript</category> <description>Your AI agent will confidently tell a customer they&#39;re GDPR-compliant when they&#39;re not. Here&#39;s the architecture that fixes that.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/22/your-ai-agent-does-not-know-the-law-and-how-to-fix-that/</link> <pubDate>Mon, 22 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/22/your-ai-agent-does-not-know-the-law-and-how-to-fix-that/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/blog/compliant-agents/infographic.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>The Zombie in the Basement</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>Software Architecture</category> <category>ai-agents</category> <category>architecture</category> <category>documentation</category> <category>graph</category> <category>migrations</category> <category>verification</category> <description>Static architecture catalogs document what someone knew at a point in time. Betonix GmbH thought they had 23 database connections. They had 52. The migration nearly shipped that delta.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/19/the-zombie-in-the-basement/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/19/the-zombie-in-the-basement/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/blog/architectural-x-ray/slide-01.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Google&#39;s Open Knowledge Format and the problems it deliberately doesn&#39;t solve</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>ai-agents</category> <category>google</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>knowledge-format</category> <category>okf</category> <category>temporal</category> <category>trust</category> <description>Google&#39;s Open Knowledge Format standardises content packaging for agents but stops before trust, temporality, and governance. KCP covers exactly that territory.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/17/googles-open-knowledge-format-and-the-problems-it-deliberately-doesnt-solve/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 17 Jun 2026 18:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_created.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/17/googles-open-knowledge-format-and-the-problems-it-deliberately-doesnt-solve/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/blog/okf-kcp/okf-kcp-slide-04.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item>